Chapter: Interval

 The shared interval-arithmetic kernel for the compiler's two range
 provers. The checker's aexpr-proven-range consumes it (through
 range-interval, TypeCheckerInference); the emitter's
 ir-expr-proven-range still carries a private, hand-written copy that
 refuses everything but non-negative operands, so on that side a named
 local holding a provably small signed value still has no provable
 range. This chapter is the single kernel both
 provers will consume, written for full signed i64 semantics and
 verified by an exhaustive containment test
 (codex/test/interval-exhaustive.codex) in the ConstantRange
 discipline: every transfer function states its soundness contract in
 prose, and the test checks that contract against the runtime's own
 operators for every interval over a small universe. The kernel is
 pure arithmetic over a two-field record; it allocates one record per
 call and nothing else.

Section: Representation

  Interval = record {
   lo : Integer,
   hi : Integer
  }

  iv-i64-max : Integer = 9223372036854775807

  iv-i64-min : Integer = 0 - 9223372036854775807 - 1

  iv-unknown : Interval = Interval { lo = iv-i64-min, hi = iv-i64-max }

  iv-empty : Interval = Interval { lo = 1, hi = 0 }

  iv-is-unknown : Interval -> Boolean
  iv-is-unknown (a) = a.lo == iv-i64-min & a.hi == iv-i64-max

  iv-is-empty : Interval -> Boolean
  iv-is-empty (a) = a.lo > a.hi

  iv-exact : Integer -> Interval
  iv-exact (n) = Interval { lo = n, hi = n }

Section: Overflow Refusal

 Predicates that decide whether an i64 endpoint computation would
 overflow, without themselves overflowing. Addition uses the standard
 rearrangement: a + b overflows iff b > 0 and a > max - b, or b <= 0
 and a < min - b; both right-hand sides stay in range under their
 guards. Subtraction mirrors it. Multiplication compares against a
 truncating division of the endpoint constants - Codex integer
 division truncates toward zero, which is exactly the rounding these
 bounds need - after handling the two corners where either operand is
 -1 against i64-min, because negating i64-min is itself an overflow.

  iv-add-overflows : Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  iv-add-overflows (a) (b) =
   if b > 0 then a > iv-i64-max - b
   else a < iv-i64-min - b

  iv-sub-overflows : Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  iv-sub-overflows (a) (b) =
   if b > 0 then a < iv-i64-min + b
   else a > iv-i64-max + b

  iv-mul-overflows : Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  iv-mul-overflows (a) (b) =
   if a == 0 | b == 0 then False
   else if a == 0 - 1 then b == iv-i64-min
   else if b == 0 - 1 then a == iv-i64-min
   else if b > 0 then a > iv-i64-max / b | a < iv-i64-min / b
   else a > iv-i64-min / b | a < iv-i64-max / b

  iv-int-min : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  iv-int-min (a) (b) = if a <= b then a else b

  iv-int-max : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  iv-int-max (a) (b) = if a >= b then a else b

Section: Addition

  iv-add : Interval, Interval -> Interval
  iv-add (a) (b) =
   if iv-is-empty a | iv-is-empty b then iv-empty
   else if iv-add-overflows (a.lo) (b.lo) | iv-add-overflows (a.hi) (b.hi) then iv-unknown
   else Interval { lo = a.lo + b.lo, hi = a.hi + b.hi }

Section: Subtraction

  iv-sub : Interval, Interval -> Interval
  iv-sub (a) (b) =
   if iv-is-empty a | iv-is-empty b then iv-empty
   else if iv-sub-overflows (a.lo) (b.hi) | iv-sub-overflows (a.hi) (b.lo) then iv-unknown
   else Interval { lo = a.lo - b.hi, hi = a.hi - b.lo }

Section: Negation

  iv-neg : Interval -> Interval
  iv-neg (a) =
   if iv-is-empty a then iv-empty
   else if a.lo == iv-i64-min then iv-unknown
   else Interval { lo = 0 - a.hi, hi = 0 - a.lo }

Section: Multiplication

  iv-mul : Interval, Interval -> Interval
  iv-mul (a) (b) =
   if iv-is-empty a | iv-is-empty b then iv-empty
   else if iv-mul-overflows (a.lo) (b.lo) | iv-mul-overflows (a.lo) (b.hi) | iv-mul-overflows (a.hi) (b.lo) | iv-mul-overflows (a.hi) (b.hi) then iv-unknown
   else let p1 = a.lo * b.lo
   in let p2 = a.lo * b.hi
   in let p3 = a.hi * b.lo
   in let p4 = a.hi * b.hi
   in Interval {
    lo = iv-int-min (iv-int-min p1 p2) (iv-int-min p3 p4),
    hi = iv-int-max (iv-int-max p1 p2) (iv-int-max p3 p4)
   }

Section: Division

  iv-div : Interval, Interval -> Interval
  iv-div (a) (b) =
   if iv-is-empty a | iv-is-empty b then iv-empty
   else if b.lo <= 0 & b.hi >= 0 then iv-unknown
   else if a.lo == iv-i64-min & b.hi == 0 - 1 then iv-unknown
   else let q1 = a.lo / b.lo
   in let q2 = a.lo / b.hi
   in let q3 = a.hi / b.lo
   in let q4 = a.hi / b.hi
   in Interval {
    lo = iv-int-min (iv-int-min q1 q2) (iv-int-min q3 q4),
    hi = iv-int-max (iv-int-max q1 q2) (iv-int-max q3 q4)
   }

Section: Modulo

 SOUNDNESS CONTRACT: for every concrete pair (x, y) with x in the
 input's range, y in the other input's range, and y not zero, the
 concrete result int-mod x y lies inside the returned interval. What
 the runtime actually does (emit-int-mod-builtin in
 Emit/X86_64Builtins.codex): idiv leaves the truncated remainder,
 which carries the sign of the DIVIDEND, and a negative remainder is
 then corrected by adding the absolute value of the divisor. The
 result is therefore Euclidean - always in [0, abs(y) - 1] - for
 every sign combination, negative dividends and negative divisors
 included. The bound is [0, m] where m is the largest abs(y) - 1 over
 the divisor corners; abs(iv-i64-min) - 1 is exactly iv-i64-max, so
 the magnitude is computable at every corner. A divisor range
 containing zero answers iv-unknown, same argument as division.

  iv-mod-mag : Integer -> Integer
  iv-mod-mag (n) =
   if n == iv-i64-min then iv-i64-max
   else if n < 0 then (0 - n) - 1
   else n - 1

  iv-mod : Interval, Interval -> Interval
  iv-mod (a) (b) =
   if iv-is-empty a | iv-is-empty b then iv-empty
   else if b.lo <= 0 & b.hi >= 0 then iv-unknown
   else Interval { lo = 0, hi = iv-int-max (iv-mod-mag (b.lo)) (iv-mod-mag (b.hi)) }

Section: Union

  iv-union : Interval, Interval -> Interval
  iv-union (a) (b) =
   if iv-is-empty a & iv-is-empty b then iv-empty
   else if iv-is-empty a then b
   else if iv-is-empty b then a
   else Interval { lo = iv-int-min (a.lo) (b.lo), hi = iv-int-max (a.hi) (b.hi) }

Section: Intersection

 SOUNDNESS CONTRACT: every concrete x that lies in BOTH inputs'
 ranges lies inside the returned interval; the returned interval is
 in fact exactly the set of such x. Disjoint inputs produce the
 canonical iv-empty. An empty input needs no special case: taking the
 max of the lows and the min of the highs against any lo > hi pair
 yields lo > hi again, which normalizes to iv-empty.

  iv-intersect : Interval, Interval -> Interval
  iv-intersect (a) (b) =
   let n-lo = iv-int-max (a.lo) (b.lo)
   in let n-hi = iv-int-min (a.hi) (b.hi)
   in if n-lo > n-hi then iv-empty
   else Interval { lo = n-lo, hi = n-hi }

Section: Minimum and Maximum

  iv-min : Interval, Interval -> Interval
  iv-min (a) (b) =
   if iv-is-empty a | iv-is-empty b then iv-empty
   else Interval { lo = iv-int-min (a.lo) (b.lo), hi = iv-int-min (a.hi) (b.hi) }

  iv-max : Interval, Interval -> Interval
  iv-max (a) (b) =
   if iv-is-empty a | iv-is-empty b then iv-empty
   else Interval { lo = iv-int-max (a.lo) (b.lo), hi = iv-int-max (a.hi) (b.hi) }

